# Issue triage, part 1 of 2: CLASSIFY ONLY. No closes, no milestone edits. 2026-09-12
#
# DISPATCH: ./scripts/dispatch.sh git@github.com:Arlencho/olympus-platform.git wave-plans/triage/2026-09-12-issue-triage-classify.plan --auto --retries 2
#
# 322 open issues, 226 with no milestone, in violation of
# docs/operations/project-management.md. This plan produces a verdict list and
# STOPS. A founder reads it, then part 2 executes the closes that survived.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 | claude | Classify every open issue in Arlencho/olympus-platform that has NO milestone, and write nothing to any issue except one tracking comment at the end. READ FIRST: docs/operations/project-management.md in full, especially the three milestone definitions and the Triage recipe; then run gh issue list with state open, limit 500, and json for number, title, body, labels, createdAt, updatedAt, assignees, milestone, and keep only the ones whose milestone is null. Expect about 226. FOR EACH ONE produce exactly one verdict from this closed set: CLOSE_FIXED, CLOSE_OBSOLETE, or one of the existing milestone titles as printed by gh api repos slash Arlencho slash olympus-platform slash milestones. A verdict of CLOSE_FIXED or CLOSE_OBSOLETE requires EVIDENCE you actually checked, never inferred from the title: for CLOSE_FIXED, the merged PR number that closed it or the file and line on main where the described behaviour now exists, verified by reading main; for CLOSE_OBSOLETE, the specific fact that makes it moot, such as the surface it describes having been deleted in a named PR, or a newer issue that supersedes it by number. If you cannot name evidence, the verdict is a milestone, not a close. An issue whose body describes something you can see is still broken on main is never a close. RULES YOU MUST FOLLOW. One, the stale-audit trap: an issue can look live and be fully fixed, or look fixed and be live; the only defence is opening the cited file on main, so do it, and note in your evidence that you did. Two, issues assigned to luddement are classified for milestone only and never proposed for close; they are his. Three, issues labelled human-task, or with a co-founder decision pending, get a milestone and a note, never a close. Four, do not propose closing anything that carries an open PR. Five, the fabricated-data class is not syntactic: an issue about invented content is fixed only when the rendering-side invariant exists, not when a grep for the string is empty. OUTPUT: post ONE comment on the issue you create for this sweep, titled Issue triage 2026-09-12 classification, containing a table with columns issue, current labels, verdict, evidence, one row per issue, grouped by verdict with counts at the top. Then a second section listing every issue you were unsure about with the two verdicts you considered and why, so a human can decide those. Do NOT close anything. Do NOT edit any milestone. Do NOT comment on any individual issue. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. If the run cannot finish all issues, post what you have with the count reached and stop; a partial honest list beats a complete guessed one. | main

2 | plan-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the classification posted by wave 1 on the triage tracking issue titled Issue triage 2026-09-12 classification. No closes, no edits, no comments on any other issue. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: would executing these verdicts close something that is still real. Sample strictly: take EVERY row marked CLOSE_FIXED or CLOSE_OBSOLETE, not a subset, and for each one open the cited evidence yourself. A CLOSE_FIXED whose cited PR did not merge or whose cited file and line does not show the fix on main is a false close; a CLOSE_OBSOLETE whose superseding issue is itself closed or does not cover the same defect is a false close. Report the false-close count and list every one by number with what you found. Then sample 20 of the milestone verdicts at random and check the milestone fits the definition in project-management.md. Then check the rules: no luddement-assigned issue proposed for close, no human-task proposed for close, nothing with an open PR proposed for close. Post ONE comment on the same tracking issue whose first line reads CRITIC TRIAGE CLASSIFICATION, with the false-close list, the rule violations, and a verdict of SAFE-TO-EXECUTE for the close set minus your false-close list, or BLOCK if the false-close rate exceeds one in ten, since that rate means the classification cannot be trusted at all. No vendor names. | main
